//! lotus/stereo_pad_tail.rye -- ALES151: the padded top-and-tail carried into stereo -- trim the leading and trailing JOINT silence like ALES150, yet keep a chosen
//! margin of joint silence adjacent to the content at each end, on both channels in lockstep. ALES150 `stereo_top_tail` cuts the joint dead air at both ends flush
//! against the first and last frame either speaker fills. That is right for a sample library and wrong for a spoken take -- a cut that lands exactly on the first
//! consonant clips the attack and reads abrupt. Every recorder and DAW that trims silence also lets a keeper KEEP A MARGIN, so the take breathes. ALES99 added that one
//! honest parameter to the mono trimmer, `pad`; this rung carries it into stereo. The flush stereo trim is exactly pad = 0; every larger pad keeps that much of the
//! joint dead air back, bounded by how much joint silence each end holds.
//!
//! The wall is the one ALES148, ALES149, and ALES150 all met: a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips whose defining invariant is that LEFT and RIGHT hold
//! the SAME length, and ALES147 proved the two channels segment INDEPENDENTLY -- their silences differ in count and boundary. Padding each channel by its OWN silence
//! would give the two speakers DIFFERENT margins and DIFFERENT lengths and tear the stereo image apart. So this rung keeps ONE margin for BOTH: the leading and trailing
//! JOINT silence (a run of frames `.silent` in both channels), kept up to `pad` and cut for the excess, identically from both channels.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: segment BOTH channels through ALES147 over one shared frame grid, find the FIRST and LAST frame that is NOT jointly-silent (the content
//! bracket, the union of the two speakers' outer content, exactly ALES150's), keep up to `pad` samples of the leading and trailing joint silence and cut only the
//! EXCESS from both channels -- the trailing excess first (a suffix cut shifts nothing before it), then the leading excess. Every interior pause and the kept margin stay
//! untouched, on both channels.
//!
//! The crux -- two laws, each tying this rung to a proven predecessor so it can never drift. THE PAD-ZERO LAW: stereo_top_and_tail_pad(sc, ..., 0) equals ALES150's
//! stereo_top_and_tail(sc, ...) byte for byte on both channels -- the flush stereo trim is exactly the zero-margin case. THE MARGIN LAW: the result equals the original
//! over [content_start - min(pad, lead_joint_silence), content_end + min(pad, tail_joint_silence)) byte for byte on both channels -- the kept margin at each end is
//! exactly min(pad, available), a pad larger than the available joint silence keeps all of it and no more (never inventing a sample), a pad at least as large as both
//! ends leaves the master unchanged; monotone in pad; both channels leaving equal length.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES151.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-054529_fill-ales151-lotus-stereo-pad-tail.md
//! Stands on: lotus/stereo_top_tail.rye (ALES150) -- the flush stereo top-and-tail, the pad-zero twin, its StereoTopTailError reused whole; lotus/stereo_segment.rye
//! (ALES147) -- the stereo segmenter, run once over one shared frame grid, its segment_stereo, its StereoSegments; lotus/segment.rye (ALES95) -- the mono segmenter and
//! max_segments, through ALES147; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the StereoClip and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip and its cut; lotus/voiced.rye
//! (ALES94) -- the .silent verdict this intersects.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It edits only by removing whole
//! excess-jointly-silent frames at the two ends through ALES2's own cut, on both channels in lockstep, fabricating no sample and reordering nothing, never touching a
//! sample between the first and last not-jointly-silent frames or within the kept margin. NOT a transcript, not a real adaptive VAD; no real sample rate, no network,
//! no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_pad_tail.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const segment = @import("segment.rye");
const stereo_segment = @import("stereo_segment.rye");
const stereo_top_tail = @import("stereo_top_tail.rye");

pub const stereo_pad_tail_version = "20260815.054529";

/// The padded stereo trimmer's faults are ALES150's OWN faults (the stereo segmenter's StereoSegmentError and the editor's EditError) reused by name -- it invents none
/// of its own, and `pad` is a plain count with no illegal value (a huge pad simply keeps all available joint silence).
pub const StereoPadTailError = stereo_top_tail.StereoTopTailError;

/// Trim the leading and trailing JOINT silence from both channels of `sc` like ALES150, yet keep up to `pad` samples of joint silence adjacent to the content at each
/// end. Segments BOTH channels of the whole clip `[0, len)` into frames of `frame_len` samples through ALES147, finds the first and last frame NOT jointly-silent, keeps
/// `min(pad, available)` of the joint silence at each end, and cuts only the excess from BOTH channels -- the trailing excess first (so the leading index stays valid),
/// then the leading excess. Validates the whole span, band, floor, and frame length through ALES147's own checks BEFORE any cut; on refusal both channels are untouched.
/// `pad = 0` is exactly ALES150. An empty clip refuses `BadFrame`; an all-jointly-silent master trims to empty whatever the pad; a no-joint-silent master is unchanged.
pub fn stereo_top_and_tail_pad(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    frame_len: u32,
    t_low: i32,
    t_high: i32,
    silence_floor: u32,
    voice_split: u32,
    pad: u32,
) StereoPadTailError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced -- the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the whole edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    const original_len: u32 = sc.left.len;

    // Segment BOTH channels once over one shared frame grid. ALES147 runs all of its (and ALES95's) validation here -- band/span precheck, the floor ceiling, the
    // dividing frame length, the empty-clip BadFrame -- read-only, before a single sample is cut. On refusal it leaves the source untouched, so both channels are too.
    var segs = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
    try stereo_segment.segment_stereo(sc, &segs, 0, original_len, frame_len, t_low, t_high, silence_floor, voice_split);

    // invariant: segmenting a non-empty clip yields at least one run per channel that tiles it -- ALES147's own postcondition, relied on here.
    assert(segs.left.len >= 1 and segs.left.len <= segment.max_segments);
    assert(segs.right.len >= 1 and segs.right.len <= segment.max_segments);

    // The frames tile [0, original_len) exactly -- the segmenter refused a non-dividing or zero frame length, so this division is exact and at least one.
    const frames: u32 = original_len / frame_len;
    // invariant: the frame count is real and at least one.
    assert(frames >= 1);

    // Forward pass -- find the FIRST and LAST frame NOT jointly-silent, the content bracket. Walk the frames, advancing one cursor per channel across its own tiling run
    // list, and mark a frame jointly-silent only where it is `.silent` in both channels. `found` stays false only when every frame is jointly silent.
    var found: bool = false;
    var first_ns: u32 = 0;
    var last_ns: u32 = 0;
    var li: u32 = 0;
    var ri: u32 = 0;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < frames) : (k += 1) {
        const fs: u32 = k * frame_len;
        while (li + 1 < segs.left.len and segs.left.items[li].start + segs.left.items[li].count <= fs) : (li += 1) {}
        while (ri + 1 < segs.right.len and segs.right.items[ri].start + segs.right.items[ri].count <= fs) : (ri += 1) {}
        // invariant: each cursor names the run that actually covers fs -- the runs tile the span, so exactly one always does.
        assert(segs.left.items[li].start <= fs and fs < segs.left.items[li].start + segs.left.items[li].count);
        assert(segs.right.items[ri].start <= fs and fs < segs.right.items[ri].start + segs.right.items[ri].count);
        const joint_silent: bool = segs.left.items[li].verdict == .silent and segs.right.items[ri].verdict == .silent;
        if (!joint_silent) {
            if (!found) {
                first_ns = k;
                found = true;
            }
            last_ns = k;
        }
    }

    if (!found) {
        // ALL JOINTLY SILENT: no content to keep a margin around -- the whole master is dead air, so it trims to empty regardless of pad (a margin needs a sound beside).
        // invariant: cutting the whole span leaves each channel empty; the two cuts are identical, so balance holds.
        try timeline.cut(&sc.left, 0, original_len);
        try timeline.cut(&sc.right, 0, original_len);
        // invariant: both channels are empty when this runs, so the padding is proven to be all
        // that follows rather than laid over samples still there
        assert(sc.left.len == 0 and sc.right.len == 0);
        return;
    }

    // The content bracket runs from the first not-jointly-silent frame to the last -- the frames tile the span, so every frame before first_ns is jointly-silent (the
    // leading joint silence) and every frame after last_ns is jointly-silent (the trailing joint silence).
    const content_start: u32 = first_ns * frame_len; // first content sample
    const content_end: u32 = (last_ns + 1) * frame_len; // one past the last content sample
    // invariant: the content bracket sits inside the clip.
    assert(content_start <= content_end and content_end <= original_len);

    const lead_silence: u32 = content_start; // jointly-silent samples before the content
    const tail_silence: u32 = original_len - content_end; // jointly-silent samples after the content

    // Keep min(pad, available) of the joint silence at each end; cut only the excess. The kept margin is identical on both channels because the joint silence is shared.
    const lead_keep: u32 = if (pad < lead_silence) pad else lead_silence;
    const tail_keep: u32 = if (pad < tail_silence) pad else tail_silence;
    const lead_start: u32 = content_start - lead_keep; // kept span start (content_start minus the kept lead margin)
    const tail_stop: u32 = content_end + tail_keep; // kept span end (content_end plus the kept tail margin)
    // invariant: the kept span brackets the content and lies inside the clip.
    assert(lead_start <= content_start and content_end <= tail_stop and tail_stop <= original_len);

    // Cut the TRAILING excess first (from tail_stop to the clip's end) from BOTH channels. A suffix cut shifts nothing before it, so the leading index stays valid for
    // the next cut; the same span cut from both keeps them balanced.
    const tail_cut: u32 = original_len - tail_stop;
    if (tail_cut > 0) {
        // invariant: the trailing excess lies wholly at each clip's end.
        assert(tail_stop + tail_cut == sc.left.len and tail_stop + tail_cut == sc.right.len);
        try timeline.cut(&sc.left, tail_stop, tail_cut);
        try timeline.cut(&sc.right, tail_stop, tail_cut);
    }

    // Cut the LEADING excess (from the start, `lead_start` samples) from BOTH channels. The kept span shifts down to the front, interior pauses and the kept margins
    // intact; the same span cut from both keeps them balanced.
    if (lead_start > 0) {
        // invariant: the leading excess still lies at the front of each channel -- the tail cut touched only samples after it.
        assert(lead_start <= sc.left.len and lead_start <= sc.right.len);
        try timeline.cut(&sc.left, 0, lead_start);
        try timeline.cut(&sc.right, 0, lead_start);
    }

    // invariant: both channels hold exactly the content plus the two kept margins, and leave balanced.
    assert(sc.left.len == tail_stop - lead_start);
    assert(sc.right.len == tail_stop - lead_start);
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE PAD-ZERO LAW -- stereo_top_and_tail_pad(pad=0) equals ALES150's stereo_top_and_tail byte for byte on both channels; THE MARGIN LAW -- the kept
//     margin at each end is exactly min(pad, available), the result equal to the original over the padded span per channel, both channels balanced; a known pad keeps
//     exactly the named samples; a pad past both ends leaves the master unchanged; monotone in pad; an all-jointly-silent master trims to empty whatever the pad; a
//     no-joint-silent master is unchanged; an empty clip, a ragged frame length, an inverted band, and an illegal floor each refuse by name, both channels untouched --

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

/// The verdict of the frame that starts at sample `fs`, read from a mono segmentation by finding the run that covers it -- an independent reference to the cursor walk
/// inside stereo_top_and_tail_pad, so the crux is proven against a second implementation.
fn frame_verdict(segs: *const segment.Segments, fs: u32) segment.Segment {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < segs.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (segs.items[i].start <= fs and fs < segs.items[i].start + segs.items[i].count) return segs.items[i];
    }
    unreachable; // the runs tile the span, so a frame start always lands in one
}

/// The hand-built expectation the padded stereo trimmer is checked against in the margin law -- segment BOTH channels of the ORIGINAL, find the first and last frame NOT
/// jointly-silent, and copy the original's samples over the padded span [content_start - min(pad, lead), content_end + min(pad, tail)) on each channel. An all-jointly-
/// silent master leaves `out` empty.
fn by_hand(out: *pan.StereoClip, src: *const pan.StereoClip, frame_len: u32, t_low: i32, t_high: i32, floor: u32, split: u32, pad: u32) !void {
    var ls = segment.Segments{};
    try segment.segment(&src.left, &ls, 0, src.left.len, frame_len, t_low, t_high, floor, split);
    var rs = segment.Segments{};
    try segment.segment(&src.right, &rs, 0, src.right.len, frame_len, t_low, t_high, floor, split);
    const frames: u32 = src.left.len / frame_len;
    var found: bool = false;
    var first_ns: u32 = 0;
    var last_ns: u32 = 0;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < frames) : (k += 1) {
        const fs: u32 = k * frame_len;
        const l_silent = frame_verdict(&ls, fs).verdict == .silent;
        const r_silent = frame_verdict(&rs, fs).verdict == .silent;
        if (!(l_silent and r_silent)) {
            if (!found) {
                first_ns = k;
                found = true;
            }
            last_ns = k;
        }
    }
    if (!found) return; // all jointly silent — nothing kept
    const content_start = first_ns * frame_len;
    const content_end = (last_ns + 1) * frame_len;
    const lead_silence = content_start;
    const tail_silence = src.left.len - content_end;
    const lead_keep = if (pad < lead_silence) pad else lead_silence;
    const tail_keep = if (pad < tail_silence) pad else tail_silence;
    const start = content_start - lead_keep;
    const end = content_end + tail_keep;
    try timeline.splice(&out.left, 0, src.left.samples()[start..end]);
    try timeline.splice(&out.right, 0, src.right.samples()[start..end]);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const band_low: i32 = -1000;
    const band_high: i32 = 1000;

    // Six frames of four samples, the two channels carrying DIFFERENT silences, joint-silent iff BOTH quiet. Leading joint silence 4 samples (frame 0), trailing joint
    // silence 4 samples (frame 5). Content bracket [4, 20): first not-joint frame 1 (left loud), last not-joint frame 4 (right loud); frame 2 is an interior joint gap.
    const left_sig = [_]i16{
        40,    -40,    40,     -40, // frame 0 — quiet both (leading joint silence)
        20000, 20000,  -20000, -20000, // frame 1 — loud (first content)
        30,    -30,    30,     -30, // frame 2 — quiet both (interior gap)
        20000, 20000,  -20000, -20000, // frame 3 — loud
        45,    -45,    45,     -45, // frame 4 — quiet (right loud keeps it)
        50,    -50,    50,     -50, // frame 5 — quiet both (trailing joint silence)
    };
    const right_sig = [_]i16{
        41,    -41,    41,     -41, // frame 0 — quiet
        42,    -42,    42,     -42, // frame 1 — quiet (left loud keeps it)
        31,    -31,    31,     -31, // frame 2 — quiet both (interior gap)
        43,    -43,    43,     -43, // frame 3 — quiet (left loud keeps it)
        20000, 20000,  -20000, -20000, // frame 4 — loud (last content)
        51,    -51,    51,     -51, // frame 5 — quiet
    };

    // 1. THE PAD-ZERO LAW -- over many frame lengths, floors, and splits, stereo_top_and_tail_pad(pad=0) equals ALES150's flush stereo_top_and_tail on both channels.
    {
        const flens = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24 };
        const floors = [_]u32{ 100, 500, 1000, 5000 };
        const splits = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 4 };
        var lci: u32 = 0;
        while (lci < flens.len) : (lci += 1) {
            var fi: u32 = 0;
            while (fi < floors.len) : (fi += 1) {
                var si: u32 = 0;
                while (si < splits.len) : (si += 1) {
                    var padded = pan.StereoClip{};
                    try load_stereo(&padded, &left_sig, &right_sig);
                    var flush = pan.StereoClip{};
                    try load_stereo(&flush, &left_sig, &right_sig);
                    try stereo_top_and_tail_pad(&padded, flens[lci], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si], 0);
                    try stereo_top_tail.stereo_top_and_tail(&flush, flens[lci], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si]);
                    if (!eq(padded.left.samples(), flush.left.samples())) return error.PadZeroLeftNotFlush;
                    if (!eq(padded.right.samples(), flush.right.samples())) return error.PadZeroRightNotFlush;
                    if (padded.left.len != padded.right.len) return error.PadZeroUnbalanced;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // 2. THE MARGIN LAW -- over many pads, the padded trim equals the hand-built padded span per channel, both channels balanced, the kept margin exactly min(pad, avail).
    {
        const pads = [_]u32{ 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 100 };
        var pi: u32 = 0;
        while (pi < pads.len) : (pi += 1) {
            var got = pan.StereoClip{};
            try load_stereo(&got, &left_sig, &right_sig);
            var src = pan.StereoClip{};
            try load_stereo(&src, &left_sig, &right_sig);
            var want = pan.StereoClip{};
            try stereo_top_and_tail_pad(&got, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, pads[pi]);
            try by_hand(&want, &src, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, pads[pi]);
            if (!eq(got.left.samples(), want.left.samples())) return error.LeftNotThePaddedSpan;
            if (!eq(got.right.samples(), want.right.samples())) return error.RightNotThePaddedSpan;
            if (got.left.len != got.right.len) return error.MarginUnbalanced;
        }
    }

    // 3. A KNOWN PAD -- with frame 4, floor 500, pad 2: content is [4, 20), each end holds 4 joint-silent samples, so 2 are kept at each end. Result = original[2, 22) =
    //    20 samples per channel: the last 2 leading joint-silent samples, the content bracket (with its interior gap), and the first 2 trailing joint-silent samples.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &left_sig, &right_sig);
        try stereo_top_and_tail_pad(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 2);
        const want_left = [_]i16{
            40,    -40, // 2 kept leading joint-silent samples (of 4)
            20000, 20000,  -20000, -20000, // frame 1
            30,    -30,    30,     -30, // frame 2 interior gap, kept
            20000, 20000,  -20000, -20000, // frame 3
            45,    -45,    45,     -45, // frame 4
            50,    -50, // 2 kept trailing joint-silent samples (of 4)
        };
        const want_right = [_]i16{
            41,    -41,
            42,    -42,    42,     -42,
            31,    -31,    31,     -31,
            43,    -43,    43,     -43,
            20000, 20000,  -20000, -20000,
            51,    -51,
        };
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &want_left)) return error.KnownPadLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &want_right)) return error.KnownPadRightWrong;
        if (sc.left.len != 20 or sc.right.len != 20) return error.KnownPadLength;
    }

    // 4. A PAD PAST BOTH ENDS leaves the master unchanged -- pad 4 keeps all 4 leading and all 4 trailing joint-silent samples, and there is no joint silence beyond them.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &left_sig, &right_sig);
        try stereo_top_and_tail_pad(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 4);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &left_sig) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &right_sig)) return error.FullPadChanged;
    }

    // 5. MONOTONE IN PAD -- a larger margin keeps both channels at least as long (and still balanced).
    {
        const pads = [_]u32{ 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 8 };
        var prev: u32 = 0;
        var pi: u32 = 0;
        while (pi < pads.len) : (pi += 1) {
            var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
            try load_stereo(&sc, &left_sig, &right_sig);
            try stereo_top_and_tail_pad(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, pads[pi]);
            if (sc.left.len < prev) return error.NotMonotone;
            if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.MonotoneUnbalanced;
            prev = sc.left.len;
        }
    }

    // 6. AN ALL-JOINTLY-SILENT master trims to empty whatever the pad (a margin needs a sound to sit beside).
    {
        const quiet_l = [_]i16{ 40, -40, 40, -40, 30, -30, 30, -30 };
        const quiet_r = [_]i16{ 41, -41, 41, -41, 31, -31, 31, -31 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &quiet_l, &quiet_r);
        try stereo_top_and_tail_pad(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 3);
        if (sc.left.len != 0 or sc.right.len != 0) return error.AllJointSilentNotEmpty;
    }

    // 7. A NO-JOINT-SILENT master is unchanged -- no joint silence at either end to trim or keep. The right is loud throughout, so nothing is jointly silent.
    {
        const some_l = [_]i16{
            40, -40, 40, -40, 20000, -20000, 20000, -20000, // quiet then loud on the left
        };
        const loud_r = [_]i16{
            20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, 20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // loud throughout on the right
        };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &some_l, &loud_r);
        try stereo_top_and_tail_pad(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 1, 2);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &some_l)) return error.NoJointSilentLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &loud_r)) return error.NoJointSilentRightChanged;
    }

    // 8a. AN EMPTY CLIP refuses BadFrame, both channels untouched and balanced.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        if (stereo_top_and_tail_pad(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 2)) |_| return error.EmptySlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFrame) return error.WrongEmpty;
        if (sc.left.len != 0 or sc.right.len != 0) return error.EmptyMutated;
    }

    // 8b. A RAGGED FRAME LENGTH refuses BadFrame, both channels untouched.
    {
        const five_l = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
        const five_r = [_]i16{ 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &five_l, &five_r);
        if (stereo_top_and_tail_pad(&sc, 2, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 2)) |_| return error.RaggedSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFrame) return error.WrongRagged;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &five_l) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &five_r)) return error.RaggedMutated;
    }

    // 8c. AN INVERTED BAND refuses BadThreshold, both channels untouched.
    {
        const four_l = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40 };
        const four_r = [_]i16{ 50, 60, 70, 80 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &four_l, &four_r);
        if (stereo_top_and_tail_pad(&sc, 2, 1000, -1000, 500, 2, 2)) |_| return error.InvertedSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadThreshold) return error.WrongInverted;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &four_l) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &four_r)) return error.InvertedMutated;
    }

    // 8d. AN ILLEGAL FLOOR refuses BadFloor, both channels untouched and balanced.
    {
        const four_l = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40 };
        const four_r = [_]i16{ 50, 60, 70, 80 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &four_l, &four_r);
        if (stereo_top_and_tail_pad(&sc, 2, band_low, band_high, 32769, 2, 2)) |_| return error.BadFloorSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFloor) return error.WrongBadFloor;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &four_l) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &four_r)) return error.BadFloorMutated;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.BadFloorUnbalanced;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-pad-tail: the PADDED TOP-AND-TAIL CARRIED INTO STEREO — trim the leading and trailing JOINT silence like ALES150, yet keep a chosen " ++
        "margin of joint silence adjacent to the content at each end, on both channels in lockstep. ALES150 cuts the joint dead air flush against the first and last " ++
        "frame either speaker fills; a flush cut clips a spoken take's attack, so every recorder and DAW lets a keeper keep a margin. ALES99 added that one honest " ++
        "parameter to the mono trimmer, pad; this carries it into stereo. The wall is the one ALES148/149/150 met — a StereoClip's defining invariant is that left and " ++
        "right hold the SAME length, yet ALES147 proved the two channels segment INDEPENDENTLY, so padding each channel by its own silence would give the two speakers " ++
        "different margins and lengths and tear the stereo image apart. So this rung keeps ONE margin for BOTH: the leading and trailing JOINT silence, kept up to pad " ++
        "and cut for the excess, identically from both channels. The rule: segment both channels through ALES147, find the FIRST and LAST frame NOT jointly-silent " ++
        "(the content bracket, the union of the two speakers' outer content, exactly ALES150's), keep min(pad, available) of the leading and trailing joint silence " ++
        "and cut only the excess from both channels (trailing first, so the leading index stays valid). Every interior pause and the kept margin stay untouched on " ++
        "both channels. The crux is two laws. THE PAD-ZERO LAW: stereo_top_and_tail_pad(pad=0) equals ALES150's flush stereo_top_and_tail byte for byte on both " ++
        "channels — the flush trim is exactly the zero-margin case. THE MARGIN LAW: the result equals the original over [content_start - min(pad, lead_joint_silence), " ++
        "content_end + min(pad, tail_joint_silence)) byte for byte on both channels — the kept margin at each end is exactly min(pad, available), a pad past the " ++
        "available joint silence keeps all of it and no more (never inventing a sample), a pad past both ends leaves the master unchanged; monotone in pad; both " ++
        "channels balanced. A known pad keeps exactly the named samples; an all-jointly-silent master trims to empty whatever the pad; a no-joint-silent master is " ++
        "unchanged; an empty clip, a ragged frame length, an inverted band, and an illegal floor each refuse by name — BadFrame, BadFrame, BadThreshold, BadFloor — " ++
        "both channels untouched. Purely local — it edits only by removing whole excess-jointly-silent frames at the two ends through cut on both channels in " ++
        "lockstep, fabricating no samples and reordering nothing; NOT a transcript or a real adaptive VAD; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no " ++
        "real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
